WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.100
Security headers need operational ownership, not one-time copy-paste.

2
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:15.000
ASP.NET Core applications often run behind proxies, ingress controllers, load balancers, CDNs, web servers, and hosting platforms.

3
00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:18.940
Any of those layers can add, remove, or change headers.

4
00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:23.920
The organization should know which layer owns each policy and how changes are reviewed.

5
00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:26.040
Review where headers are applied.

6
00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:31.180
Application middleware may cover MVC and API responses but not all static files.

7
00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:36.000
A CDN may cover cached assets but not dynamic error responses.

8
00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:41.300
A reverse proxy may handle redirects or HSTS before the app sees traffic.

9
00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:49.960
Authentication pages, admin pages, health endpoints, file downloads, and exception paths may each need separate confirmation.

10
00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:52.000
Environment behavior matters.

11
00:00:52.100 --> 00:01:06.500
Local development, staging, production, preview deployments, and branch deployments often use different hosts, proxies, certificates, CDNs, origins, and third-party dependencies.

12
00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:12.120
A header that works locally may be too broad, too narrow, or absent in production.

13
00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:15.340
Release review should compare the environments that matter.

14
00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:21.300
CSP often benefits from report-only rollout when enforcement risk is high.

15
00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:31.320
Report-only behavior can reveal missing sources, legacy inline patterns, third-party dependencies, and browser differences before blocking user workflows.

16
00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:37.660
That reporting should have an owner and a cleanup plan; collecting reports without review does not improve the policy.

17
00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:43.380
Track exceptions with owners, reasons, review dates, and environment scope.

18
00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:51.400
Exceptions may be legitimate, especially for legacy pages, partner integrations, analytics, embedded content, or staged migrations.

19
00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:54.860
But unexplained exceptions become permanent weak spots.

20
00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:59.040
Governance keeps the header baseline from drifting as the application changes.

21
00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:01.640
Avoid scanner-driven copy-paste hardening.

22
00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:09.100
Scanners can be useful prompts, but they do not know the application's business flows, deployment layers, or compatibility constraints.

23
00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:16.560
A professional header baseline explains why each header exists, where it is applied, what value is expected, and who maintains it.
